@AThousandShips Yes I agree, I was originally going to go with a different name until I found the intentional 10-character hard limit to keywords in gdscript, at which point I couldn't think of anything else good which both contained `assert` + a 3-letter max word. I didn't test it but the limit seems to be easily changeable, although it was intentionally put there so I've avoided doing that.

Some other names I came up with in not that much time, both of which are over 10 characters are `assert_release` and `assert_always`.

If adding an optional boolean argument to `assert` to make it run in release builds is better than a new keyword I think I could do that too.

@AThousandShips If you try to compile with anything over 10 characters it fails due to the intentionally-set maximum.

https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/2487a297b236e28e8ddf96fa8d6b44a164c7cb2c/modules/gdscript/gdscript_tokenizer.cpp#L549

I assume the purpose is to avoid having people try to implement keywords which are too long (essentially what my original plan was), so I just left it.

@dalexeev I am now realizing that this pr really makes more sense in the context of multiplayer. In my code, I am constantly emulating the functionality of `assert`, including the hidden control flow. If the `assert` is hit while I have a debugger connected, I do want the breakpoint / error log so that I can figure out the issue and restart the game because this is an issue with my code. However, with rpcs malicous peers can also choose to send values which can crash the game, which is why I want the method to also return in release builds.

In my mind I guess I also imagined that a return value for failure could be specified with an `assert` some day. Currently I can replace at more than half of my `push_error` + `breakpoint` + `return`s in my codebase with this keyword without changing the return values or behaviour of the methods, so I guess my code tends to work well with using default values as failure results?

Reading the arguments in the discussion you linked does make this seem less in-line with what `assert` is intended to be used for, because the problem the users are having is that they want to hit an `assert` and continue using the program as normal, but that is also what this pr is assuming.
